{"seconds": 45.3, "exit": 0, "final": "Excellent! So Kc6 forces either:\n1. Rxc7, and White plays Kxc7, winning the rook (material +5)\n2. Bd7+, and White plays Kxd7, attacking the rook on c8, which is now undefended (winning +3 for the bishop, and then the pawn promotes)\n\nThis is clearly winning. Let me write the coaching note and verify the main line one more time:\n\n**Best move: Kc6**\n\n**Main line continuation:**\n1. Kc6 Rxc7 (forced, or else c8=Q)\n2. Kxc7 Kxe5\n3. b5 (advancing passed pawns)\n\n**Motif: Deflection** (or perhaps **trappedPiece** for the rook on c8)\n\n**Explanation:**\nYour c7 pawn is a powerhouse one square from promotion. By playing Kc6, you control the key escape square for Black's rook while supporting your pawn. Black cannot take c7 profitably (after Rxc7, Kxc7 wins the rook outright), and if Black tries to interpose with Bd7+, you capture it and the rook on c8 is still trapped. The key insight: before moving your pieces forward, check if your passed pawn's promotion square is already becoming undefendable for your opponent. A pawn on the 7th rank supported by the king is often unstoppable.\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Kc6\", \"line\": [\"Kc6\", \"Rxc7\", \"Kxc7\", \"Kxe5\", \"b5\"], \"motifs\": [\"trappedPiece\", \"quietMove\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "2r5/p1P5/3Kb3/4Bkp1/1P6/6P1/8/8 w - - 4 60"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "2r5/p1P5/3Kb3/4Bkp1/1P6/6P1/8/8 w - - 4 60"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "2r5/p1P5/3Kb3/4Bkp1/1P6/6P1/8/8 w - - 4 60", "square": "c8"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "2r5/p1P5/3Kb3/4Bkp1/1P6/6P1/8/8 w - - 4 60", "square": "e6"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "2r5/p1P5/3Kb3/4Bkp1/1P6/6P1/8/8 w - - 4 60", "square": "e5"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "2r5/p1P5/3Kb3/4Bkp1/1P6/6P1/8/8 w - - 4 60"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-i